## P0 — Launch blockers

### P0.1 Device-code approval is a bypass of the web/cli token split

Problem: a stolen web session (XSS, malware, shared PC) can start `kekkai login` on the attacker's
machine, approve the code from the hijacked browser session, and receive a `cli` token. That token
can call `/sync/pull` and read everything, so the "dashboard can never reveal secrets" guarantee
collapses.

Hardened flow:

```
 Attacker/User CLI            API                       Browser (logged in)
 ─────────────────            ───                       ───────────────────
 kekkai login ───────────►  create device_code (hashed)
                            user_code = 8 chars, high
                            entropy, expires in 10 min
 ◄── shows user_code + URL
                                                        open /cli/authorize
                                                        ┌───────────────────────────┐
                                                        │ Enter code shown in your   │
                                                        │ terminal: [ ABCD-EFGH ]    │
                                                        │ Requested from: IP, city,  │
                                                        │ OS, device name, time      │
                                                        │ STEP-UP: password or TOTP  │
                                                        │ (fresh, < 5 min old)       │
                                                        └───────────────┬───────────┘
                            approve only if step-up valid ◄─────────────┘
                            email notification: "New CLI device authorized"
 poll ◄── cli token (bound to device record: name, IP, created, last used)
```

Requirements:
- The user must type the code into the browser (not click a link that pre-fills it).
- Approval requires step-up auth (password re-entry, or TOTP once MFA exists) even in a live session.
- Show device metadata on the approval screen; single-use codes; 10-minute expiry; max 5 wrong
  attempts then the code is burned; rate limit `/cli/authorize` per user and per IP.
- Send an email on every new CLI device. Provide a "not me → revoke all sessions" link.
- Store CLI tokens as device records so the dashboard can list and revoke them.
- Verify `tokenType` is never accepted from client input on any endpoint.
- Tests: approval without step-up fails; expired, reused and brute-forced codes fail.

### P0.2 Audit must fail closed for plaintext access

Fire-and-forget audit is fine for metadata reads, not for value reads. For `/reveal`, `/sync/pull`
and `get`: write the audit row first (same transaction where possible); if the write fails, return
500 and do not return plaintext. Test it by forcing the audit insert to throw.

### P0.3 Bind ciphertext to its context (AAD)

Use AES-256-GCM additional authenticated data so a DB writer cannot swap ciphertexts between rows
or projects.
- Secret value AAD = `projectId | environmentId | secretId | key | version`.
- DEK wrap AAD = `projectId | kekVersion`.
- Since there is no production data yet, change it now; write the migration/re-encrypt note anyway.
- Test: moving a ciphertext to another secret row must fail authentication.

### P0.4 Real authorization tests (not only crypto unit tests)

Add integration tests with a real test DB and supertest:
- Route enumeration: iterate the Express router and assert every route that can return a secret
  value returns 403 for a `web` token and 401 without a token. Fail CI if a new route is not
  classified as `metadata`, `cli-only`, or `public`.
- IDOR: user B requests user A's project/environment/secret IDs on every endpoint → 403/404.
- RBAC matrix (role × endpoint × environment) as a table-driven test. VIEWER never receives values.
- Cross-project `clone`: requires read on source AND ADMIN+ on destination.
- Refresh rotation: replaying an old refresh token revokes the whole family.
- Log test: run the whole suite with a canary secret value and grep captured stdout/stderr.

### P0.5 Environment-level permissions

Roles alone are too coarse. Add per-environment policy:
- `production` (and any environment flagged `protected`): value access limited to OWNER/ADMIN by
  default; DEVELOPER access is an explicit per-member grant.
- `pull` and `run` on protected environments require a fresh step-up (or `--confirm-env production`).
- Show these rules in the dashboard's Team page.

### P0.6 Service tokens for CI/CD

Without these, people will paste personal CLI tokens into CI. Add a third token type `service`:
- Scoped to one project + one environment, read-only by default, mandatory expiry (max 90 days).
- Created via `kekkai token create --env production --ttl 30d --name github-actions`, shown once,
  stored hashed, revocable, every use audit-logged (with IP).
- CLI accepts `KEKKAI_TOKEN` env var for non-interactive use; service tokens can only call `pull`/`run`.

### P0.7 Account security

- Email verification before an account can create projects.
- TOTP MFA (with recovery codes). Require MFA for OWNER/ADMIN of any project with a `production`
  environment, and for CLI device approval.
- Password policy: minimum length 12, breached-password check (HIBP k-anonymity API), no composition
  rules theatre.
- Per-account lockout/backoff (not only per-IP rate limits), generic error messages so login and
  register cannot be used to enumerate emails. Split login and register into separate endpoints if
  the current "login creates account if username present" behavior still exists.
- Session/devices page: list web sessions and CLI devices, revoke individually or all.

### P0.8 CLI credential storage (you are on Windows)

`~/.kekkai/config.json` with `0600` means nothing on Windows.
- Store refresh tokens in the OS keychain (Windows Credential Manager / macOS Keychain / libsecret)
  via a maintained keyring package; fall back to a file only with an explicit warning and
  best-effort permissions (`icacls` on Windows).
- Config file keeps non-secret data only (API URL, active project/env IDs).
- `kekkai doctor` reports which storage backend is in use.

### P0.9 Rate limiting that survives production

- Move limiter state to Redis (Upstash is fine); in-memory limits reset on deploy and don't span instances.
- Set Express `trust proxy` correctly for Render/Railway so IP limits don't key on the proxy.
- Key limits on user ID where authenticated, IP where not. Raise/split limits so `kekkai run` in
  normal dev use never trips them; service tokens get their own bucket.

